WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.680
Shane Stanley, Filmmaker/Author/Instructor:&nbsp;
Everybody who's ever invested in me has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:05.440
told me no. Everybody. Nobody has&nbsp;
rolled over and said “Absolutely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:08.240
let's do it.” It's always come&nbsp;
from people who have said no.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:13.600
Film Courage: If a filmmaker's going to raise&nbsp;
money and we should just probably pick some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:19.920
ballpark figure so maybe $500,000?&nbsp;
How can a filmmaker mitigate risk?

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:26.320
Shane: I think when when putting together a half&nbsp;
a million dollar budget the best way to mitigate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:32.640
risk on that is to think about genre think&nbsp;
about star power as I as I try to tell a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:39.280
of producers if you if you're only thinking&nbsp;
about what the public perception is of your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:44.400
work and the stars that you are working with is&nbsp;
your end game you're not never going to be happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:52.400
be smart in your casting just because somebody is&nbsp;
not a-list studio powerhouse player today doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:58.080
mean they weren't 5-10 years ago doesn't mean&nbsp;
somebody isn't on three television series that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:04.160
syndicating around the globe two or three times&nbsp;
a day in 140 different countries those actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:12.160
are gold and they're not expensive so when when we&nbsp;
when we cast our films it's about familiar faces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:16.720
it's about people with good reputation people that&nbsp;
we enjoy being with 18 hours a day anyway right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:22.160
but I think from mitigating risk it's about&nbsp;
it's about getting actors that people recognize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:27.840
and people can say oh I remember this actor&nbsp;
from that series or this series or that show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:34.320
or that film and it's when you're working in a&nbsp;
half a million dollars it's really not a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:44.400
money it goes fast and to be able to put maybe 35&nbsp;
40 of that above the line I think is is smart and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:50.400
then you you got your your crew and all below&nbsp;
the line make sure that's an efficient team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:55.600
that makes your product look and sound good get&nbsp;
good editors do whatever you have to do to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.560
the product look as good as it can I mean you can&nbsp;
make that that money go a long way if how to do it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:07.920
but again it comes down to content a five hundred&nbsp;
thousand dollar drama without the right cast is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.520
not the same thing as a 300 000 action film with&nbsp;
just one or two right people in it I mean there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:20.480
they can be completely different success so I&nbsp;
always think it goes back to really understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:27.120
globally what your product how how products of&nbsp;
that budget and that level and similar cast do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:30.640
it's really important to look at like films&nbsp;
I mean as filmmakers again it goes back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:33.600
thinking everything we do is so special&nbsp;
we're going to knock the world on its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.720
behind because this is just the best script&nbsp;
ever and it's going to be the best movie ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:42.720
I always say always go in under promise over&nbsp;
deliver and I think that's the biggest problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:49.520
with a lot of the pitch decks that we look&nbsp;
at that come through everybody's got names&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.560
and faces on these things they're never going&nbsp;
to acquire and they comp it to films they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:58.880
not even ever going to come close to comping&nbsp;
with they always pick the big grand slam films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:04.400
and and what I try to teach producers is that&nbsp;
when you go in with all these promises and hype&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:08.640
you're going to start casting your film and you're&nbsp;
going to have two strikes against you when your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:13.040
your investor says well who's in it and you didn't&nbsp;
get the names of the faces that were in your pitch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:20.800
deck and I think that a lot of people go in such&nbsp;
high expectations and they get reality check sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:24.480
you're going to get the occasional like wow I&nbsp;
can't believe we got this person or that person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:30.400
but when when you're pitching to investors I&nbsp;
always say let's just remember the investors are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:34.880
smarter than we are we're going to them for money&nbsp;
because they are successful at doing things and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:41.680
it's very similar to real estate if somebody came&nbsp;
to you and said I want you to put 500 grand into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:46.800
this housing project and within 16 months you're&nbsp;
going to make 20 million dollars you probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:52.640
wouldn't invest especially with an accredited&nbsp;
builder developer or somebody that and see the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:56.800
track record that okay their half million dollars&nbsp;
turns into 20 million within a year and a half and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:01.360
a lot of filmmakers go in with that kind of pomp&nbsp;
and circumstance what I like to do when I pitch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:06.560
investors is I use films that most people haven't&nbsp;
heard of is comps where I say hey this film was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:12.880
made for 500 grand it made back 3 million this&nbsp;
film was made for 600 grand it made back 7 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:16.560
because I'm not going to compare it to Blair Witch&nbsp;
I'm not going to compare it to Napoleon Dynamite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.480
that stuff's all lightning in a bottle and and&nbsp;
good on them there's nothing no ding against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:27.680
them it's just that's reality and investors sniff&nbsp;
that bull a mile away I think the most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:31.760
thing when you're pitching people for money is&nbsp;
is you go and you make the film as low as you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:34.880
because the first thing an investor always wants&nbsp;
to know is how much money are you getting paid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:38.880
we talked about that in some of our earlier&nbsp;
interviews I think how much do you pay yourself or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:44.320
what shouldn't it what should you be paid and the&nbsp;
old saying is the the producer is always the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:48.880
one to buy lunch and the last to get paid right I&nbsp;
think investors like to see you're not lining your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:54.400
pockets they want to know you have certain skin in&nbsp;
the game and when you go to them and you say look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.400 --> 00:05:00.720
you're $500,000 investment if we place it here&nbsp;
here and here with this star power could generate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.520
two to five million dollars over the next three to&nbsp;
five years all of a sudden they don't feel they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:10.800
getting a snow job and that's that's the reality&nbsp;
of our business and that's if you're doing well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:16.240
so I think what's really important is it's you&nbsp;
ask about finding the sweet spot on budget I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:20.080
think it's about I think you always have to put&nbsp;
your investor first because a lot of people just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:24.000
want to get one movie made and they figure they're&nbsp;
going to move on to the next and where we've been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:28.960
very successful is having a track record with&nbsp;
with investors we don't do one-off investors we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:33.680
work with the same people over and over again and&nbsp;
that comes from trust it comes from delivering it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:37.120
comes from putting a product on the screen&nbsp;
that they could be proud of when it's done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:45.840
and and having success globally and it takes time&nbsp;
nothing happens like that and and that's I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:52.240
the problem is is all these pitch decks that come&nbsp;
in are just lined with bull and fluff and hype&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:57.440
and I think if people just take a step back&nbsp;
and kind of do a reality check and what they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:01.200
presenting I think they'd be a lot more successful&nbsp;
in getting funds it's just about honesty and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:07.200
integrity and you said also not just the images&nbsp;
and in the pitch text but also shooting something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:10.800
like a scene that goes so much further than I&nbsp;
look at some of these POCS [Proof of Concept] that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:15.600
people shoot and they just look they look so good&nbsp;
I I've never been impressed with the pitch deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:21.200
and it's just littered it's littered with&nbsp;
garbage. I just got sent a 60-page pitch deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:25.040
that I'm consulting a producer&nbsp;
on next week he sent it to me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:31.280
I shot holes in 95 of it and they spent a lot of&nbsp;
money getting this pitch deck made it is useless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:35.440
and it's really unfortunate because I see it all&nbsp;
the time and it's not that I have all the answers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:41.040
I've raised a lot of money from private equity&nbsp;
that's what I do I know when they know they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:47.360
getting garbage and I know what not to do and&nbsp;
these pitch decks are just constantly just just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:51.840
filled with so much information it's paperweight&nbsp;
that they don't need less I'm a big I'm a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:57.120
proponent of less is more in the room I talk about&nbsp;
it in the book uh the first time I ever raised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:01.760
private equity I went into a very successful&nbsp;
businessman's office got took three months to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:06.400
the meeting I brought in a briefcase filled with&nbsp;
scripts budgets we didn't have the term pitch deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:10.400
back then but head shots and what we're gonna do&nbsp;
and all the projections and the ask and takes from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:15.360
the distributors and all the people that wanted to&nbsp;
sign on and I literally I literally broke out this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:21.280
this Kinkos box if you guys remember what that is&nbsp;
Kinko's I think it's Fedex printers now I broke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:26.560
out a box and a guy said stop what are you giving&nbsp;
me I said oh I have a script I have a budget he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:32.160
said look around my office and cornered a corner&nbsp;
of his grand office there was stacks of paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:38.880
knee to hip high all over the place there's just&nbsp;
paper and he said these are all proposals from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:43.840
people that want money from me he said you want to&nbsp;
end up in a pile give me your paperwork why don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:47.120
we just sit here and have a conversation you tell&nbsp;
me what it is you want to do how much money you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:50.800
need how long is it going to take for me to start&nbsp;
seeing a return what are you going to do with it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:55.760
and we were able to have a conversation&nbsp;
bang bang bang and that's that's how our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.760 --> 00:08:01.040
our funding comes it's not with pitch decks it's&nbsp;
not with reels it's not with anything other than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:07.040
just eye contact and honesty and integrity most of&nbsp;
our outlines when we when we meet with investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:13.120
is three to five pages tops they're really&nbsp;
small and it's just it's just facts here you go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:20.480
so then where where is the idea of of&nbsp;
these elaborate pitch decks come from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:24.960
oh I think it's brilliant I think somebody came&nbsp;
up with the idea that they make a lot of money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:30.560
getting them from filmmakers to build a pitch deck&nbsp;
I don't know and I'm sure they work I know there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:34.880
are platforms that do seminars on pitch decks and&nbsp;
I'm sure they work for people I'm not saying they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:39.440
don't work for everybody I just know that I see my&nbsp;
share of them because a lot of people send them in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:42.640
and say hey will you give me your thoughts on this&nbsp;
pitch deck and they are just loaded with garbage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:48.960
and I really believe less is more it's two plus&nbsp;
two's got to equal four in an investor's mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:52.640
a lot of people that you go to for&nbsp;
money have been pitched everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:56.880
and they they pay business managers and&nbsp;
accountants a lot of money to protect them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:00.800
and they're all told the same thing don't invest&nbsp;
in the music industry the film industry nightclubs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:05.040
or restaurants right that's like the first thing&nbsp;
when you get money you're told what not to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:10.320
so you already go in with a strike against you&nbsp;
when you meet with an investor and they've seen it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:15.120
all they've heard it all and they know that they&nbsp;
have the ability to make your dreams come true by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:20.880
just saying the three-letter word yes and I think&nbsp;
speaking too much in the room is wrong I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:26.960
even a fool is considered wise when they're quiet&nbsp;
wait for questions to be asked stay on point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:32.320
don't over analyze everything I've seen more deals&nbsp;
get killed in a room because a potential investor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:36.640
will ask a question and there's a 30-minute&nbsp;
answer I know I'm long-winded that's my nature but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:41.760
in the room it's a whole different ball game&nbsp;
yes no if they ask for something you tell them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:48.240
how it's going to be and you shut your mouth and&nbsp;
people ruin their own deals time and time again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:53.680
with too much information and as I talk about&nbsp;
in the book don't be afraid to say I don't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:58.800
one of the greatest most fruitful relationships I&nbsp;
ever had with a with an investor came from saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:03.520
I don't know he asked me a question I didn't know&nbsp;
the answer and I said sir I don't know but I'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:10.080
find out for you and what that did is allowed me&nbsp;
an excuse to contact them again in a day or two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:13.920
with the answer to the question they wanted to&nbsp;
know and I called them back I got them through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:18.160
their secretary he got on the phone he said yeah&nbsp;
what do you want and I said look really enjoyed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:21.200
our time together a couple days ago you asked&nbsp;
me a question I didn't have the answer and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:27.200
said I did I said yeah I told him this was your&nbsp;
question this is the answer he said I got to tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:32.880
you something after you left my attorney and I we&nbsp;
enjoyed our meeting with you we made fun of you in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:37.200
some respect but at your expense that happens we&nbsp;
make fun of everybody who comes in and tap dances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:42.320
for money he said but we we were really moved by&nbsp;
the fact that you said you didn't know the answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:46.400
to something because it showed us that you're&nbsp;
willing to say you don't know you were willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:50.800
to be honest enough not to talks your way out of&nbsp;
it and you took the time to find out he said well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:54.960
listen I appreciate the information I'm going to&nbsp;
let right now I'm just going to say yes to your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.960 --> 00:11:00.640
proposal let's move forward and do it that wound&nbsp;
up being like a financial relationship that went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:05.040
on for for I don't know how many films and how&nbsp;
many years and that's that was birthed out of just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:13.120
saying I don't know so when it comes to trying to&nbsp;
raise private equity it's important to remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:17.600
these people are smarter than you and they've&nbsp;
heard it all and and I they they can usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:23.120
smell right through the bull they cut to the&nbsp;
chase with them they know why you're there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:28.720
but when they said they made fun of you did they&nbsp;
tell you what some of the things were like oh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:33.840
eventually they did well you get nervous when&nbsp;
you're you're tap dancing for for your next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:38.160
meal you sometimes you tremble sometimes you say&nbsp;
things wrong I think I spilled water on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:42.560
coffee table not anything but they gave me a glass&nbsp;
of water and I think I was reaching for something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:47.360
and I spilled and it was I was nervous it's&nbsp;
like my first it was my first attempt at getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:53.120
capital I was scared it seems forgivable&nbsp;
but yeah okay yeah it was funny to them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.200 --> 00:12:01.120
I guess so let's see the the indie guy&nbsp;
squirm yeah oh my god and what's funny is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:06.400
what happens I'll tell you I'll tell you something&nbsp;
we talk about money I've had investors call back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:11.840
when they don't always say yes and they said&nbsp;
why do you need so much money people are making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:16.640
movies on a dime now it seems it doesn't seem&nbsp;
like you're blowing stuff up you're not hanging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:20.400
off the Empire State Building why do you need so&nbsp;
much money investors are getting more and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:25.600
savvy about what things really cost they become&nbsp;
very wise to it there's just so much information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:30.480
at their fingertips now the people that they&nbsp;
surround themselves with they they are paid to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:35.040
investigate and know what things really cost the&nbsp;
days of going into people and asking for millions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:39.040
of dollars or getting further and further&nbsp;
I mean there are those rare exceptions but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.040 --> 00:12:45.120
I think you also have to be really savvy on on&nbsp;
an investor's ability keep in mind what just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:48.000
because somebody's rich doesn't mean they can just&nbsp;
roll over and write you millions of dollars in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:52.800
checks you don't know what their overhead is their&nbsp;
exposure is what other responsibilities they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:59.360
what tax needs they have or have to avoid so I&nbsp;
think it's really important to if somebody is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:04.320
is introducing you to somebody that is a potential&nbsp;
investor you need to do your research if somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:07.840
is saying I want to I want to introduce you&nbsp;
to somebody they could be a potential investor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.840 --> 00:13:13.120
take the time to say what do they do what is their&nbsp;
family size what are they they got houses all over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:17.680
the world are they they live a modest lifestyle&nbsp;
are they flashy find out research them google's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:21.920
wonderful thing find out some of the things that&nbsp;
have happened in their life their successes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:27.840
their failures make sure they haven't had family&nbsp;
tragedies that is your project may remind them of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:32.080
and I'm a firm believer whenever you go into the&nbsp;
room to pitch somebody whether it's an independent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:36.480
financier or it's a studio always have&nbsp;
two or three projects under your belt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:41.760
or under your your arm because you may&nbsp;
say something and start down the road of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:45.360
project and they can say yeah no no no we're not&nbsp;
interested we don't want to make film like that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:50.720
or we're not interested we develop something like&nbsp;
that it didn't work you wait two or three months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:53.680
to get a meeting with somebody and they tell&nbsp;
you in the first two sentences of your pitch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:57.840
this isn't going to work for them that meeting&nbsp;
is either over or you can say okay respectfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:04.240
understood put it away pull out another project&nbsp;
so if they're accountants let's suppose these are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:09.520
not quote-unquote film people these usually are&nbsp;
okay so so they're people that have made money&nbsp;&nbsp;

